  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In room two-oh-five, Yulya tastes the cognac ice cream and is thrilled. Downstairs, Katya flashes an okay sign to her father, which Senya and Fedya mistake for proof that she was given the sheikh's ring.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я принесла мороженое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, вы попробуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Давайте, неверные, поторапливаемся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тащи сюда быстрее!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ну что там?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Видал, она жест показала!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Типа ей перстень дали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Просят повторить мороженое в номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Опять несут мороженое из коньяка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Точно хотят все перстни забрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас сделаю ещё одну порцию.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я принесла мороженое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I brought the ice cream.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'ice cream' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, вы попробуйте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, try it.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you.' Notice how Russian expresses 'thank you' and 'thank you' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Давайте, неверные, поторапливаемся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, infidels, let us hurry along.' The grammar packs 'let's' and 'hurry along' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тащи сюда быстрее!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bring it over here faster!' Notice how Russian expresses 'bring' and 'faster' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ну что там?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, how did it go?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'there'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Видал, она жест показала!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you see, she made a gesture!' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'to show' marks a female speaker or subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Типа ей перстень дали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Like they gave her the ring!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'to give'. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Просят повторить мороженое в номер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are asking to repeat the ice cream for the room.' In Russian, the idea of 'ask for' flows naturally into 'number' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Опять несут мороженое из коньяка!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are taking cognac ice cream again!' In Russian, the idea of 'again' flows naturally into 'cognac' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Точно хотят все перстни забрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They definitely want to take all the rings.' Notice how Russian expresses 'exactly' and 'to take away' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сейчас сделаю ещё одну порцию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will make one more portion right now.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'now' before landing on 'portion'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
